1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:27,880
On the day, the book is down 684 dollars.

7
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:31,897
Arista Networks (ANET) leads, +2.4 percent.

8
00:00:31,897 --> 00:00:35,541
ExxonMobil Holdings (XOM) lags at -3.5.

9
00:00:35,541 --> 00:00:40,586
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:40,586 --> 00:00:44,112
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

11
00:00:44,068 --> 00:00:51,619
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

12
00:00:51,619 --> 00:00:58,769
12 positions, led by APA Corporation, ticker APA, MetLife, ticker MET, Avnet, ticker AVT.

13
00:00:58,769 --> 00:01:09,694
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

14
00:01:09,656 --> 00:01:14,088
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

15
00:01:14,088 --> 00:01:16,305
Every morning OROS proposes.

16
00:01:16,305 --> 00:01:26,040
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

17
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:29,681
Inside the book today: 5 positions up, 7 down.

18
00:01:29,681 --> 00:01:31,897
Best, ANET at +2.44 percent.

19
00:01:31,897 --> 00:01:33,480
Worst, APA at -5.53.

20
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:38,071
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

21
00:01:38,071 --> 00:01:44,640
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -4.95% per trade over 340 graded positions.

22
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:51,130
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

23
00:01:51,130 --> 00:01:55,879
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

24
00:01:55,879 --> 00:02:10,443
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 214 names close the day in Stage 2; 2 new invalidations queue exit reviews; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

25
00:02:10,399 --> 00:02:12,611
Over to the sports desk.

26
00:02:12,611 --> 00:02:21,737
Day 53 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

27
00:02:21,737 --> 00:02:26,530
In first place, the index fund, in gray, at 100,369.

28
00:02:26,530 --> 00:02:33,259
Then the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, 1.5 thousand back at 98,847.

29
00:02:33,259 --> 00:02:40,357
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 3.3 thousand back at 97,022.

30
00:02:40,357 --> 00:02:42,938
The race has no finish line.

31
00:02:42,938 --> 00:02:47,178
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

32
00:02:47,178 --> 00:02:51,971
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

33
00:02:51,971 --> 00:02:53,815
Nothing is restated.

34
00:02:53,815 --> 00:03:02,722
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

35
00:03:02,684 --> 00:03:10,019
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

36
00:03:10,019 --> 00:03:12,261
Nothing here is financial advice.

37
00:03:12,261 --> 00:03:15,249
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
